hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're going to talk  about scaffold proteins so scaffold  proteins sometimes called scaffolding  proteins are essential regulators of a  lot of important signaling pathways and  you can think of scaffold proteins as  being like the glue that holds some of  these signaling pathway components  together to increase their efficiency  and effectiveness excuse me  so scaffold proteins work through four  major functions one of the things they  do is they actually tether signaling  components together in order to increase  their efficiency what do we mean by  tether we have an example here scaffold  protein and purple plasma membrane and  blue and we've got a few different types  of proteins here  so some signaling components like kinase  is and black B proteins that they are  phosphorylated or their targets and  Krane and what you can see that the  scaffold protein does is it actually can  tether certain things together to  increase their efficiency  so without the scaffold protein holding  these kindnesses and close sort of  proximity to their target proteins the  kinases they might have to move through  the entire cell will be looking looking  for their target protein the scaffold  protein holds them together so that as  soon as the kinases are activated they  can go ahead and activate the downstream  target protein that's an example of that  tethering function the next function  where scaffolding proteins are so  important is they actually will localize  the signaling components to specific  regions so in the drawing here this  scaffold protein is holding these  proteins quite close to the plasma  membrane other scaffold proteins might  localize signaling  opponents close to the nucleus or close  to a mitochondrion wherever whatever a  region of the cell they're supposed to  be acted in the scaffold proteins can  make sure that they stay there another  important function of these scaffold  proteins is to coordinate positive or  negative feedback functions what this  means is that there are various types of  feedback in the cell positive feedback  is types of feedback that amplify a an  effect negative feedback is something  that slows the process down  and so scaffold proteins can help to  regulate some of these things so for  example scaffold proteins can make sure  that signaling components like the  kindnesses are only able to activate one  downstream target rather than amplify  but rather than active fiying multiple  downstream targets and having this large  amplification scaffold proteins can kind  of keep it from getting out of control  scaffold proteins can also actually in  some cases deactivate some signaling  components once they've had a chance to  work once or twice so perhaps a kinase  phosphorylates only one or two things  and then gets inactivated by the  scaffold protein and so that would be  like a form of of negative feedback the  fourth and final function we're going to  talk about today involves the scaffold  protein actually insulating or  protecting the signaling components from  inactivation or degradation so as an  example kinase is are important  signaling proteins they they activate  downstream targets by transferring a  phosphate group to them phosphatases are  enzymes that D phosphorylate things and  that can in innocence inactivate things  and some phosphatases can actually in  activate these kinase  well scaffold proteins can protect these  kindnesses from being inactivated by  ostentatious basically just by keeping  them apart  so by insulating these components at  keeping the kinases safe from the  phosphatases the kinase has have a  chance to do their job  the phosphatases cannot inactivate them  and studies show that kinase is that are  interacting the scaffold protein are  much less likely to be be phosphorylated  by phosphatases  in in general and so these scaffold  proteins can help to protect these  signaling components and help ensure  that they can do their job and not be  inactivated so that is the sort of  important function these four main  functions of scaffold proteins and you  can see how they really are an important  regulator of various signaling pathways  if you are interested in looking at any  of my other videos you'll find videos on  positive and negative feedback also on  enzyme activity and those are ones that  you might want to check out so thanks  for watching biology professor
 

